// Embedded check-loop schedule (TASK-0048.04) — single worker + a
// real-time `check loop` frame for the tier-3 embedded-pattern backend.
//
// Identical placement to naive.sched.nuc (everything on `host`), plus a
// `check loop i : latency_max=…, on_violation=log` directive. This is the
// AC#3 fixture: it cross-compiles for thumbv7em-none-eabihf and runs in
// Renode, where the per-iteration SysTick-measured latency is checked and
// a violation emits a one-line UART message via the shim's
// report_violation hook (the no_std analogue of tier-1's eprintln!).
//
// on_violation = log (NOT panic): on tier-3 a panic bricks the device
// (PRD §6.3.5). `on_violation` defaults to panic, so the embedded backend
// REJECTS a defaulted/explicit panic check loop — this directive must
// pick log explicitly.
//
// latency_max = 1ns is deliberately tiny: under Renode (not cycle-
// accurate) we want any nonzero per-iteration SysTick reading to TRIP the
// check, so the captured UART line proves the clock advanced + the
// lowering fired end-to-end. The ns figure reported is NOT physically
// meaningful under Renode (it is the lowering-correctness + clock-advance
// behaviour being verified, not timing fidelity).
//
// NOT a tier-1 [[required]] e2e cell — this is embedded-only and would
// not run on the tier-1 backends. It is exercised by
// `just renode-embedded-check` (TASK-0048.04), not the e2e matrix.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host };

    place load_input    on host;
    place load_input_b  on host;
    place save_output   on host;
    place add           on host;

    // Real-time assertion: one iteration of the `i` loop must take
    // ≤ latency_max; on violation, log a UART line (do NOT panic — that
    // bricks the MCU on tier-3, PRD §6.3.5).
    check loop i : latency_max = 1ns, on_violation = log;
}
